---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology — How We Decide If Poland Is Safe Right Now"
  description="How Is Poland Safe Now? decides whether each Polish voivodeship is safe right now: which news it reads, how the AI classification works, how often it updates, and the limits of that process."
  path="/methodology"
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main-content" class="mx-auto flex max-w-3xl flex-col gap-6 px-4 py-8 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Methodology</h1>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">How a status is decided</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Every hour, we gather recent news from Polish and international sources — covering roughly the last two
        days — about developments relevant to Poland's regional security situation. That evidence is then reviewed
        by an AI model, which assigns each of Poland's 16 voivodeships a status based only on what the news
        actually reports for that region. Nothing is invented: if there is no relevant news for a region, its
        status simply stays calm.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        If, for any reason, that hourly review doesn't run or a region's information gets too old to trust, that
        region is marked <strong>Unknown</strong> rather than shown as calm — we would always rather say "we don't
        know" than give a false sense of safety.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Status levels</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        The scale is deliberately weighted towards the calm end. Most of Poland, most of the time, is simply going
        about its day, so routine news does not move a region up the scale, and red is kept for an actual attack.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-inside list-disc space-y-1">
        <li><strong>Calm</strong> (dark green) — nothing notable reported; the normal, everyday situation.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Low</strong> (green) — minor or indirect signals only, such as routine military exercises,
          political news, or an incident elsewhere in Poland. Nothing that changes daily life here.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Elevated</strong> (yellow) — a current situation worth paying attention to in this region, such as
          an airspace violation, a closed airport, or an official warning — with nothing striking the ground.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Critical</strong> (red) — only when something physically hit the ground here: a strike, explosion,
          crash or falling debris causing damage, casualties or wreckage. Threats, overflights and intercepted
          objects do not turn a region red.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unknown</strong> — missing, stale, conflicting, or insufficient information. Missing data always
          resolves to Unknown, never Calm.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Map data</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Voivodeship boundaries shown on the map come from Poland's official geospatial registry, published by the
        Polish government's mapping authority.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Limits</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        This is an independent information service, not an official warning system, and cannot guarantee safety.
        News coverage can be incomplete, delayed, or wrong, and an AI-assisted review can make mistakes. Always
        follow instructions from Polish authorities and RCB. For emergencies, call 112.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
